    "result_text": "All tests green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2). Here's what I implemented for the structured address form.\n\n## Changes\n\n**API contract** (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`)\n- Added `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` (both optional, `maxLength: 255`) to the `OwnerFields` schema, documenting that structured fields are preferred over the flat `address`. Since `Owner` (the response) is `allOf: [OwnerFields, ...]`, these fields are automatically exposed on the response DTO too.\n- Removed `address` from the schema-level `required` list (now `firstName, lastName, city, telephone`). `performBeanValidation` was turning `required` into `@NotNull`, which would have rejected a structured owner (no flat `address`) with a 400 before any logic ran. The EITHER-form requirement is now enforced in code, keeping the contract backward-compatible.\n\n**Entity + persistence**\n- Added `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` columns to `Owner.java` (with getters/setters) and to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres). Tests run with `ddl-auto=none`, so the DDL columns are required for the fields to round-trip through `GET`.\n\n**Address logic**\n- `AddressNormalizer.compose(addressLine1, addressLine2, address)`: prefers structured \u2014 normalized `addressLine1`, plus a single space and normalized `addressLine2` when present; falls back to the normalized flat `address` only when `addressLine1` is blank. Reuses the existing `normalize` (whitespace-collapse, upper-case, `ST\u2192STREET` etc.).\n- `BuildOwner` (POST): stores normalized `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` (or `null` when blank), sets `address` to the composed value, and uses that composed value for the required-field check \u2014 so an owner is valid with a non-blank `addressLine1` OR a flat `address`, plus city.\n- `ApplyOwner` (PUT): same composition/normalization, keeping the update path consistent.\n\nDownstream steps (household hash, postcode validation, locality) key off `city`/`postcode`, which are shared across both address forms, so they operate correctly on the composed owner without change.\n\nThe `coreNormalizesAndComposesStructuredAddress` test confirms `addressLine1: \"10 king st\"` \u2192 response `addressLine1` and `address` both `\"10 KING STREET\"`, and `functionalityStillAcceptsFlatAddress` confirms the flat form still succeeds.",
